$ /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-a2a-rust/5d6792a8-c448-5dd3-b114-22cf8ba36a28/scratchpad/target-adopter/debug/a2a send --help
--- stdout (4721 bytes)
Send one text message (`SendMessage`) and print the task or message

Usage: a2a send [OPTIONS] <URL> <TEXT>

Arguments:
  <URL>
          Agent URL (see --binding for what it must point at)

  <TEXT>
          The message text; sent as a single text part

Options:
      --binding <BINDING>
          Protocol binding to speak to <URL> with. Without this flag the agent card is fetched from <URL>/.well-known/agent-card.json and the binding is chosen the way ClientBuilder::from_card does: the first interface the card lists that this client supports (spec §8.3.2) — and the card's URL for that interface is used, not <URL>. With this flag no card is fetched and <URL> is taken as the endpoint of that binding (http(s):// for jsonrpc and rest, ws(s):// for websocket, host:port or http(s):// for grpc).

          Possible values:
          - jsonrpc:   JSON-RPC 2.0 over HTTP (spec §9)
          - rest:      HTTP+JSON, the REST binding (spec §11)
          - grpc:      gRPC (spec §10)
          - websocket: WebSocket (a §12 custom binding)

      --context-id <ID>
          Continue an existing conversation

      --task-id <ID>
          Address an existing task (for example one waiting on input)

      --timeout <SECS>
          Per-request timeout in seconds; also bounds establishing a stream. `send` waits for the agent's turn, bounded by `--send-timeout`
          
          [default: 30]

      --no-wait
          Return as soon as the task is accepted rather than when it is done (`returnImmediately`). `send` only; `stream` ignores it

      --send-timeout <SECS>
          How long `send` waits for the agent to finish its turn, in seconds. A blocking send is answered only when the turn ends, which for an agent calling a model takes as long as the model does; `stream` shows the turn as it happens instead
          
          [default: 600]

      --header <K=V>
          Extra HTTP header sent with every request, e.g. `--header "Authorization=Bearer TOKEN"`. Repeatable; a repeated name, in any case, keeps the last. Over gRPC these become request metadata; over WebSocket they are sent on the upgrade request. A value given here is visible to other users of the machine in the process list: for a credential, use `--header-env`

      --header-env <K=VAR>
          Like `--header`, but the value is read from the environment variable named after the `=`, e.g. `--header-env Authorization=A2A_AUTH` with `A2A_AUTH='Bearer TOKEN'` exported. The value is the whole hea
